    <briefdescription>
<para>Ping-pong double-buffered FBO storage for a buffer pass. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Two FBOs, one index. The orchestrator reads from <ref refid="structshader_1_1_swap_chain_1a3ffe7cd89471fb18730f0a6d5449d3f7" kindref="member">read()</ref> and writes to <ref refid="structshader_1_1_swap_chain_1a41b1ec1cdaed8dd1775d9adb73d56e97" kindref="member">write()</ref> each frame, then calls <ref refid="structshader_1_1_swap_chain_1ab690dc5817b2d5bf8eea829ed8786710" kindref="member">flip()</ref> to advance. The next frame&apos;s <ref refid="structshader_1_1_swap_chain_1a3ffe7cd89471fb18730f0a6d5449d3f7" kindref="member">read()</ref> returns what this frame wrote; downstream passes (in render order) reading from this pass&apos;s <ref refid="structshader_1_1_swap_chain_1a3ffe7cd89471fb18730f0a6d5449d3f7" kindref="member">read()</ref> see this frame&apos;s just-written output.</para>
<para>Per OpenGL spec, framebuffer writes complete and become visible to subsequent texture fetches without explicit barriers when the textures differ. Ping-pong always has different read and write textures at any given draw, so no glMemoryBarrier is needed.</para>
<para>GL thread only. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
